Fix: reset the A9 before ps7_init so a stale MMU cannot block register access

Symptom, on every step that touches memory:

    [fail] step ps_init  Memory read error at 0xF8007080. MMU section translation fault
    [fail] step write 0x40000010  Memory write error ... MMU section translation fault

xsdb reaches memory through the selected A9 core context, so accesses go
through that core's MMU. Out of reset the A9 has SCTLR.M clear and addressing
is physical, which is what the whole flow assumes. But SCTLR.M survives `stop`:
if anything ran on the core beforehand -- an ELF from an earlier session, or a
bootloader when the board is not strapped for JTAG boot -- the MMU is still
enabled and every physical address is translated through page tables that do
not describe 0xF8000000 or the PL AXI window. Halting the core does not undo
that.

The existing comment explains why `rst -system` is avoided here, and that
reasoning stands: it drops the DAP and xsdb's reconnect timeout is shorter than
a JTAG round trip over the tunnel. `rst -processor` is narrower. It resets the
A9 core only, leaving the DAP and the JTAG chain untouched, which is enough to
clear SCTLR.M.

Without this the only recovery is a manual power cycle before every run, and
the failure mode is opaque: the bitstream programs fine and then every register
access fails for reasons that have nothing to do with the design under test.

--- a/remote_agent.py
+++ b/remote_agent.py
@@ select_cpu / stop_cpu sequence
         a("""if {![ft_step select_cpu {targets -set -nocase -filter {name =~ "*A9*#0"}}]} {
     ft step ps_contexts fail {APU is present but no Cortex-A9 core context: the DAP is only half awake. Power-cycle the board and run again.}
     ft done - - -
     exit 6
 }""")
+        # Memory access below goes through this core's MMU. SCTLR.M survives a
+        # `stop`, so anything that ran earlier -- a previous ELF, or a
+        # bootloader if the board is not strapped for JTAG boot -- leaves
+        # translation enabled and every physical address faults. Resetting the
+        # core clears it. Unlike `rst -system` this leaves the DAP alone, so it
+        # is safe over XVC. Failure is not fatal: on a core that was already
+        # clean the run continues unaffected.
+        a('ft_step cpu_reset {if {[catch {rst -processor} e]} '
+          '{ ft info cpu_reset "rst -processor unavailable: $e" }}')
         # JP2 = JTAG boot means the BootROM leaves the A9 running with no FSBL.
         # ps7_init writes the PS configuration registers through the DAP, and
         # the DAP refuses register and memory access on a running core, so the
         # CPU has to be halted first -- this is the `stop` that Vitis' own
         # debug launcher issues at exactly this point.
         # Already-halted is the normal case in JTAG boot, not a failure.
         a('ft_step stop_cpu {if {[catch {stop} e] '
           '&& ![string match "*Already stopped*" $e]} { error $e }}')
